Fast GPT Optimization via Artificial Bee Colony (ABC) Algorithm
Project Status
Status: Concept and prototype plan
Implementation status: Not yet implemented or benchmarked
Purpose: Explore whether an Artificial Bee Colony (ABC)-inspired scheduler can improve selected search, retrieval, or routing tasks in a Fast GPT system.
1. Overview
This project explores a nature-inspired optimization strategy based on honeybee foraging behavior. The aim is to coordinate candidate searches, evaluate their usefulness, and redirect exploration when progress stalls.
The ABC algorithm does not automatically make language-model inference faster. Any speed improvement must be demonstrated through a working implementation and repeatable benchmarks against a baseline.
2. Objectives
Search-space exploration: Explore alternative retrieval or routing candidates.
Quality-aware selection: Rank candidates using explicit, measurable criteria.
Resource efficiency: Limit unnecessary searches, tool calls, and coordination overhead.
Latency measurement: Measure end-to-end response time, throughput, and resource use.
Reliable fallback: Return the best available candidate or use the baseline path if optimization fails.
3. ABC-Inspired Roles
Employed bees: Explore assigned candidate paths or retrieval configurations.
Onlooker bees: Select promising candidates using a defined score, such as relevance, answer quality, latency, and compute cost.
Scout bees: Generate fresh candidates when existing paths stagnate or fail to meet a threshold.
These are software roles inspired by ABC, not separate physical processors or independent AI models.
4. Proposed Architecture
Input and baseline: Receive a request and record baseline latency.
Candidate generation: Produce a bounded set of search, retrieval, or routing candidates.
Parallel evaluation (optional): Evaluate independent candidates concurrently when the cost is justified.
Scoring: Rank candidates using a documented score and quality checks.
Selection: Use the strongest valid candidate; do not trade away required accuracy merely to reduce latency.
Scout refresh: Replace repeatedly poor or stagnant candidates within a fixed budget.
Fallback: If the optimizer times out or produces no acceptable result, use the baseline system.
Telemetry: Record latency, quality, errors, CPU/GPU use, memory use, and token/tool costs.
5. Initial Scoring Model
A possible candidate score is:
score = wq * quality - wl * normalized_latency - wc * normalized_cost
Where:
quality is measured against a task-specific evaluation set.
normalized_latency is latency scaled to a documented range.
normalized_cost estimates compute or tool-call cost.
wq, wl, and wc are configurable weights.
The weights must be selected and tested on representative workloads. A candidate that fails required quality or safety checks must be rejected regardless of its speed score.
6. Prototype Plan
Phase 1 — Baseline
Choose one bounded task, such as retrieval candidate ranking.
Implement or identify a baseline without ABC.
Create a repeatable test set and measure latency and quality.
Phase 2 — ABC Scheduler
Generate a small, fixed number of candidates.
Implement employed-bee exploration, onlooker selection, and scout replacement.
Set strict time, memory, and candidate-count limits.
Phase 3 — Evaluation
Compare the ABC version with the baseline using:
median and p95 latency;
throughput;
retrieval or answer quality;
CPU/GPU and memory use;
timeout and error rates;
total compute and tool-call cost.
Run multiple trials and document hardware, model, dataset, and configuration. Report improvements only if results are repeatable and quality remains acceptable.
Phase 4 — Integration
Integrate only if benchmarks show a useful improvement. Keep a feature flag and a fallback to the baseline.
7. Risks and Limitations
ABC may add overhead and make small tasks slower.
Parallel evaluation may increase memory use and contention.
Better retrieval routing does not necessarily speed up the language model's token generation.
Quality scores can be misleading unless evaluated against representative test cases.
No performance improvement is claimed until measurements are available.
